  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1226 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nAt midday, rain and clouds had cleared the area with the trough axis \nnow to our east. A large surface ridge continues to build into the \narea from the north today. The pressure gradient between the \ndeparting front and the building ridge keep winds gusty into the \nafternoon. Winds eventually become light this evening and nearly \ncalm overnight. Radiational cooling tonight could allow for patchy, \nshallow fog development if temperatures drop low enough. NBM is on \nthe cooler side of guidance on lows tonight in the mid 20s, but that \nappears to be a good forecast given the clear skies, light winds and \nCAA currently happening. Chilly fall temperatures stick around \nthrough the early part of the workweek with the ridge dominating our \npattern. Tuesday still looks to be the coldest day with the highest \nsfc pressure in place, with temperatures likely struggling to reach \n40 degrees. Luckily, winds look to remain light through this period.\n\nA mid-level trough axis approaches from the west on Tuesday, \neventually evolving into a more distinctive area of low pressure \nover the southern Plains while another low advances southward from \nthe northern Plains. Moisture availability will remain very limited \nat that time as the northern low moves over. A few models try to \ndevelop light QPF in far northeastern KS Wednesday, but forecast \nsoundings show quite a bit of dry air around 850 mb. Have kept POPs \nless than 10 percent. Northwest flow continues aloft through the \nweek, but southwesterly sfc winds return on Friday ahead of a sfc \ntrough. Temperatures will begin a warming trend, returning to the \n50s for the end of the week and for the upcoming weekend. And while \ndeterministic models continue to show varying solutions, confidence \nremains high that precipitation stays away through next weekend.",
